@anon.984efe779a4077b66be6415f964eeb42.2332 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [4348 x i8] }> <{ [4348 x i8] c"A WebAssembly plugin.\0A\0ATypst is capable of interfacing with plugins compiled to WebAssembly. Plugin\0Afunctions may accept multiple [byte buffers]($bytes) as arguments and return\0Aa single byte buffer. They should typically be wrapped in idiomatic Typst\0Afunctions that perform the necessary conversions between native Typst types\0Aand bytes.\0A\0APlugins run in isolation from your system, which means that printing,\0Areading files, or anything like that will not be supported for security\0Areasons. To run as a plugin, a program needs to be compiled to a 32-bit\0Ashared WebAssembly library. Many compilers will use the\0A[WASI ABI](https://wasi.dev/) by default or as their only option (e.g.\0Aemscripten), which allows printing, reading files, etc. This ABI will not\0Adirectly work with Typst. You will either need to compile to a different\0Atarget or [stub all functions](https://github.com/astrale-sharp/wasm-minimal-protocol/blob/master/wasi-stub).\0A\0A# Plugins and Packages\0APlugins are distributed as packages. A package can make use of a plugin\0Asimply by including a WebAssembly file and loading it. Because the\0Abyte-based plugin interface is quite low-level, plugins are typically\0Aexposed through wrapper functions, that also live in the same package.\0A\0A# Purity\0APlugin functions must be pure: Given the same arguments, they must always\0Areturn the same value. The reason for this is that Typst functions must be\0Apure (which is quite fundamental to the language design) and, since Typst\0Afunction can call plugin functions, this requirement is inherited. In\0Aparticular, if a plugin function is called twice with the same arguments,\0ATypst might cache the results and call your function only once.\0A\0A# Example\0A```example\0A#let myplugin = plugin(\22hello.wasm\22)\0A#let concat(a, b) = str(\0A  myplugin.concatenate(\0A    bytes(a),\0A    bytes(b),\0A  )\0A)\0A\0A#concat(\22hello\22, \22world\22)\0A```\0A\0A# Protocol\0ATo be used as a plugin, a WebAssembly module must conform to the following\0Aprotocol:\0A\0A## Exports\0AA plugin module can export functions to make them callable from Typst. To\0Aconform to the protocol, an exported function should:\0A\0A- Take `n` 32-bit integer arguments `a_1`, `a_2`, ..., `a_n` (interpreted as\0A  lengths, so `usize/size_t` may be preferable), and return one 32-bit\0A  integer.\0A\0A- The function should first allocate a buffer `buf` of length\0A  `a_1 + a_2 + ... + a_n`, and then call\0A  `wasm_minimal_protocol_write_args_to_buffer(buf.ptr)`.\0A\0A- The `a_1` first bytes of the buffer now constitute the first argument, the\0A  `a_2` next bytes the second argument, and so on.\0A\0A- The function can now do its job with the arguments and produce an output\0A  buffer. Before returning, it should call\0A  `wasm_minimal_protocol_send_result_to_host` to send its result back to the\0A  host.\0A\0A- To signal success, the function should return `0`.\0A\0A- To signal an error, the function should return `1`. The written buffer is\0A  then interpreted as an UTF-8 encoded error message.\0A\0A## Imports\0APlugin modules need to import two functions that are provided by the runtime.\0A(Types and functions are described using WAT syntax.)\0A\0A- `(import \22typst_env\22 \22wasm_minimal_protocol_write_args_to_buffer\22 (func (param i32)))`\0A\0A  Writes the arguments for the current function into a plugin-allocated\0A  buffer. When a plugin function is called, it\0A  [receives the lengths](#exports) of its input buffers as arguments. It\0A  should then allocate a buffer whose capacity is at least the sum of these\0A  lengths. It should then call this function with a `ptr` to the buffer to\0A  fill it with the arguments, one after another.\0A\0A- `(import \22typst_env\22 \22wasm_minimal_protocol_send_result_to_host\22 (func (param i32 i32)))`\0A\0A  Sends the output of the current function to the host (Typst). The first\0A  parameter shall be a pointer to a buffer (`ptr`), while the second is the\0A  length of that buffer (`len`). The memory pointed at by `ptr` can be freed\0A  immediately after this function returns. If the message should be\0A  interpreted as an error message, it should be encoded as UTF-8.\0A\0A# Resources\0AFor more resources, check out the\0A[wasm-minimal-protocol repository](https://github.com/astrale-sharp/wasm-minimal-protocol).\0AIt contains:\0A\0A- A list of example plugin implementations and a test runner for these\0A  examples\0A- Wrappers to help you write your plugin in Rust (Zig wrapper in\0A  development)\0A- A stubber for WASI" }>, align 1
